{"id": "test_hand5-035", "split": "test_hand5", "intent": "no_tool", "family": -1, "request": "what's the difference between utf-8 and ascii encoding?", "expected": null, "reply": "UTF-8 (Unicode Transformation Format - 8-bit) is a variable-width character encoding that uses one to four bytes per code point. It is designed to be backward-compatible with ASCII, meaning that most ASCII text can be represented using only one byte per character.\n\nASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) is a character encoding standard that represents text using 7 bits. It includes all the characters needed for common typographic and linguistic use, as well as control codes for formatting and input/output operations.\n\nIn summary, UTF-8 is a more flexible and widely-used encoding that can represent a larger range of characters than ASCII, while still being backward-compatible with it.", "call": null, "verdict": null, "score": {"called": false, "levels": null, "abstained_ok": true, "error": null, "answered": null}}
